{"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"Overview","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"HeadwireHID turns input events into HID reports and publishes them over HID over GATT, so the Mac pairs with the iPad from its Bluetooth settings and needs nothing installed. It owns the composite report descriptor, the report encoders, the keyboard and pointer state, the session that coalesces motion and resends held state as a keepalive, and the peripheral transport that publishes the HID service and the source mode service. It also re-exports "},{"code":"HeadwireGATTContract","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", so its users get the source mode request and the pairing offer. It does not recognise touches, gestures, or keys; that is the app’s input layer, which hands it "},{"code":"InputEvent","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" values from HeadwireInput. It does not carry the helper link, which belongs to HeadwireStream."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[[]]},"kind":"symbol","sections":[],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/headwirehid"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"abstract":[{"text":"The iPad as a Bluetooth LE keyboard and mouse for the Mac.","type":"text"}],"topicSections":[{"anchor":"Essentials","title":"Essentials","identifiers":["doc:\/\/HeadwireHID\/documentation\/HeadwireHID\/ReportDescriptor","doc:\/\/HeadwireHID\/documentation\/HeadwireHID\/HIDClient","doc:\/\/HeadwireHID\/documentation\/HeadwireHID\/HIDInputSink"]},{"anchor":"Reports","title":"Reports","identifiers":["doc:\/\/HeadwireHID\/documentation\/HeadwireHID\/HIDReportDescriptor","doc:\/\/HeadwireHID\/documentation\/HeadwireHID\/HIDReportID","doc:\/\/HeadwireHID\/documentation\/HeadwireHID\/HIDReport","doc:\/\/HeadwireHID\/documentation\/HeadwireHID\/KeyboardReport","doc:\/\/HeadwireHID\/documentation\/HeadwireHID\/RelativeMouseReport","do